---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-hyperparathyroidism`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Hyperparathyroidism',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Hyperparathyroidism', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E21', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Hyperparathyroidism', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Hyperparathyroidism: Primary vs Secondary Explained"
  description="Vitamin D and parathyroid hormone are locked together. Why deficiency drives secondary hyperparathyroidism, why vitamin D is often low in primary hyperparathyroidism, and when repletion is safe."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-hyperparathyroidism", label: "Vitamin D & Hyperparathyroidism" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Hyperparathyroidism</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Parathyroid hormone (PTH) and vitamin D run the calcium economy together, so when one is
      disturbed the other almost always moves. Understanding the direction of that relationship is
      what separates a harmless, self-correcting picture from one that needs surgery — and it changes
      whether taking vitamin D is helpful or hazardous.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Secondary hyperparathyroidism: deficiency is the cause</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      When 25(OH)D falls, the gut absorbs less calcium, blood calcium tends to drift down, and the
      parathyroid glands respond exactly as designed — they raise PTH to pull calcium from bone and
      conserve it in the kidney. This is <strong>secondary</strong> hyperparathyroidism: the glands
      are behaving normally in response to a deficiency. Calcium is usually normal or low. PTH begins
      to climb as 25(OH)D falls below roughly 30 ng/mL and rises steeply under 20 ng/mL — one of the
      reasons those thresholds are used. Here, correcting vitamin D <em>lowers</em> PTH and protects
      the skeleton. Chronic kidney disease produces its own, more complex form of secondary
      hyperparathyroidism (see <a href="/vitamin-d-and-kidney-disease" class="text-amber-700 underline">CKD</a>).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Primary hyperparathyroidism: the gland is the problem</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      In <strong>primary</strong> hyperparathyroidism, an adenoma (or hyperplasia) autonomously
      over-secretes PTH regardless of calcium, so blood calcium is <em>high</em> with an
      inappropriately high or high-normal PTH. Vitamin D is often low in these patients, partly
      because high PTH accelerates conversion of 25(OH)D to active 1,25(OH)₂D. For years clinicians
      feared that giving vitamin D would worsen hypercalcaemia — but trials (e.g. Rolighed 2014) show
      that cautious repletion in mildly hypercalcaemic patients lowers PTH and improves bone density
      without dangerous calcium rises, provided calcium is monitored. It should still be done under
      supervision, not self-directed.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Reading the labs</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>High calcium + high PTH</strong> → primary hyperparathyroidism (or familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia).</li>
      <li><strong>Low/normal calcium + high PTH + low 25(OH)D</strong> → secondary, from vitamin D deficiency.</li>
      <li><strong>High calcium + low PTH</strong> → look elsewhere: malignancy, granulomatous disease, or vitamin D excess.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The practical takeaway</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A raised PTH is not a reason to avoid vitamin D — most of the time it is a sign you need it,
      because deficiency is driving the gland. But a raised <em>calcium</em> changes everything: it
      means the parathyroid picture must be characterised before dosing, because a minority have
      primary disease or another cause of hypercalcaemia in which unsupervised high-dose vitamin D is
      unwise. Anyone with an elevated calcium should have it worked up rather than reaching for a
      supplement.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> If your blood calcium is elevated, do not start or continue
      high-dose vitamin D without medical review — the cause needs to be identified first. Secondary
      hyperparathyroidism from deficiency, by contrast, generally improves with proper repletion.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-hyperparathyroidism" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
